; law_layout folds every input from test/inputs through the layout, so it needs
; the parse as well: a formatter's laws are about what it does to a tree, and a
; recovered tree is the one its fixed point never held on before.

(tests
 (names law_layout)
 (modules law_layout)
 (libraries
  lingo.layout
  lingo.plan
  lingo.interp
  lingo.core
  lingo_grammars
  lingo_runtime
  lingo_runtime.ir
  handsome
  inputs
  lex
  siesta
  sweep))

; law_sample draws from the system the translation builds and parses what comes
; back, so it needs the interpreter as well as the sampler. The parse is the
; oracle for part (c): the sequences of kinds it accepts are the language, and
; the system's structure count has to be that number.

; The grammar list law_sample and law_fuzz both draw from. A library rather
; than a module of either, because a module belongs to one stanza and two
; lists of grammars are two corpora that drift apart.

(library
 (name sample_corpus)
 (modules sample_corpus)
 (libraries lingo.core lingo_grammars lingo_witness))

(tests
 (names law_sample)
 (modules law_sample)
 (libraries
  lingo.sample
  lingo_witness
  lingo.plan
  lingo.interp
  lingo.core
  lingo_grammars
  lingo_runtime
  lingo_runtime.ir
  bolts
  lex
  sample_corpus
  siesta))

; law_fuzz draws the grammars law_sample draws, and rust and effekt beside
; them. What it adds is the mutation, the coverage and the oracles; what it
; shares is the corpus list above.

(tests
 (names law_fuzz)
 (modules law_fuzz)
 (libraries
  lingo.fuzz
  lingo.sample
  lingo.core
  lingo_grammars
  lingo_runtime
  lex
  sample_corpus
  siesta))

; law_textmate reads the emitted JSON back and walks it. The walk is its own:
; the reference resolution, the capture counter and the scope-path check are
; written a second time there, because a law that calls the function it checks
; proves that the function agrees with itself.

(tests
 (names law_textmate)
 (modules law_textmate)
 (libraries
  lingo.core
  lingo.scopes
  lingo_editors.textmate
  lingo_grammars
  editor_corpus
  redfa
  yojson))

; law_treesitter reads the emitted JavaScript and queries back as text and
; walks them. Reading the text is the point: what tree-sitter is handed is a
; file, and a law that walked the emitter's own values would not see a rule
; that never reached the page.

(tests
 (names law_treesitter)
 (modules law_treesitter)
 (libraries
  lingo.core
  lingo.scopes
  lingo_editors.treesitter
  lingo_grammars
  editor_corpus
  redfa))

; law_docs reads the emitted page back. Part (f) is the one to keep: colouring
; a code example must not change it, and the only way to say so is to take the
; markup off again and compare the bytes.

(tests
 (names law_docs)
 (modules law_docs)
 (libraries
  lingo.core
  lingo.scopes
  lingo.docs
  lingo_grammars
  editor_corpus
